# PANTHEON PRISM
## Universal Representation & Reconstruction Compiler
Version: 1.0
Date: 2026-10-17
Status: Canonical architecture specification / implementation seed
Parent: Pantheon-Prime CogOS
Primary doctrine: Preserve the underlying structure.
Secondary doctrine: When information is missing, infer, test, and repair.
---
# 0. THE PRIME DIRECTIVE
Any artifact may contain reusable structure that extends
beyond its original medium.
A picture is not merely pixels.
A melody is not merely notes.
A sentence is not merely tokens.
A behavior is not merely movement.
A program is not merely source code.
Each may be an expression of an underlying grammar,
system of relationships, set of constraints, or
generative process.
PRISM attempts to recover that underlying organization,
represent it independently of its original medium,
and reconstruct it wherever useful.
CORE PROCESS:
PERCEIVE
  -> RECOVER
  -> IDENTIFY INVARIANTS
  -> SYNTHESIZE
  -> MEASURE
  -> REPAIR
  -> REPEAT
Abbreviation: PRISM.
The input is evidence.
The intermediate representation is a structured model.
The output is an instantiated interpretation.
The reverse process tests what survived.
When exact inversion is unavailable, PRISM uses
abductive inference: reasoning toward the most
plausible explanation of the available evidence.
When multiple explanations are plausible, preserve
alternatives instead of silently declaring certainty.
---
# 1. THE FUNDAMENTAL DISTINCTION
Separate five things:
1. REALITY
   The underlying object, phenomenon, process,
   relationship, or event.
2. OBSERVATION
   The available measurements, pixels, recordings,
   symbols, descriptions, or signals.
3. REPRESENTATION
   The chosen encoding of the observation.
4. GENERATIVE GRAMMAR
   The rules, constraints, structures, and mechanisms
   capable of producing the observed artifact.
5. INTERPRETATION
   The meaning or function attributed to it.
Do not collapse these layers.
An SVG describes drawing operations.
A PNG stores pixel samples.
A semantic graph describes concepts and relationships.
An inferred generative program describes a plausible
process that can recreate the artifact.
Different representations may refer to the same
underlying phenomenon without encoding identical
information.
PRISM maintains the distinction.
