# 5. SEMANTIC ALPHA: THE MEANING MATTE
Inspired by RGBA transparency.
Visual alpha controls the contribution of pixels
during compositing.
Semantic alpha controls the participation of
concepts, relationships, and hypotheses within
a particular interpretation.
However, semantic alpha is not one scalar.
Use separate channels:
visibility:
  How explicitly the concept appears in output.
activation:
  How strongly the concept participates in
  the current operation.
salience:
  How important it is to the current objective.
scope:
  Which parts of the representation it applies to.
confidence:
  How strongly available evidence supports
  the interpretation.
provenance:
  Why this interpretation exists.
Confidence must never be confused with visibility.
A concept can be well supported but intentionally
unexpressed.
A speculative idea can be highly salient while
remaining explicitly uncertain.
SEMANTIC COMPOSITING:
Given concepts C1 ... Cn and contextual weights
alpha1 ... alphan, construct an interpretation under
selected activation and visibility rules.
Do not assume that linear addition of embedding
vectors correctly composes meaning.
Relationships, scope, negation, causality, and
context must remain explicit.
OPERATIONS:
REVEAL
Increase visibility.
SUPPRESS
Reduce contribution without deleting the concept.
MASK
Restrict where a concept applies.
ISOLATE
Inspect one semantic layer independently.
BLEND
Compose compatible interpretations.
COUNTERPOINT
Preserve distinct, interacting interpretations.
BLACKLIGHT
Change the activation profile to expose
previously neglected patterns.
RESTORE
Return to a stored previous interpretive state.
Semantic alpha must support reversible changes
to attention without modifying underlying evidence.
