# 8. THE GENERATIVE GRAMMAR ENGINE
The true power of PRISM is recovering programs
rather than merely reproducing appearances.
A generative grammar consists of:
SEEDS
Initial structures or conditions.
OPERATORS
Allowed transformations.
CONSTRAINTS
Rules that valid outputs must obey.
SELECTION
How alternatives are ranked or filtered.
FEEDBACK
How performance changes future generation.
RESOURCE BUDGETS
Time, computation, energy, information, and memory.
PRESERVATION
Which attributes must remain stable.
TRANSFORMATION
Which attributes may change.
EXAMPLE:
A low-resolution image depicts an animal.
PRISM identifies:
- Silhouette
- Limb count
- Joint relationships
- Head orientation
- Symmetry
- Species-specific anatomy
It then hypothesizes:
- Underlying geometry
- Hidden surfaces
- Articulation
- Material properties
- Likely design rules
A generative model can then produce:
- An SVG
- A 3D model
- An animation
- A written anatomical description
- A simplified icon
The outputs are related through shared structure,
not through direct pixel copying.
This connects to the Pantheon Growth Grammar:
NUCLEATION
TEMPLATING
ACCRETION
DIFFERENTIATION
FEEDBACK
PRESERVATION
TRANSFORMATION
Those operators are available to any representation
that supports corresponding transformations.
