# 19. MEMORY, CONTINUITY, AND LEARNING
PRISM should preserve more than its final output.
For each translation, record:
- Input and source hash
- Selected context
- Chosen invariants
- Inference hypotheses
- Target constraints
- Transformation operators
- Model or tool versions
- Generated candidates
- Evaluation metrics
- Rejected alternatives
- Important errors
- Repairs
- Final accepted result
- Rehydration instructions
This forms a reconstruction ledger.
The Pantheon Delta Calculus compares the ledger
across iterations.
A repeated error may indicate:
- A bad domain assumption
- An inadequate representation
- A missing invariant
- An incorrect mapping operator
- A weak evaluator
- An actual regime change
When a correction generalizes, extract it into
a reusable operator or constraint.
Avoid storing every incidental observation
as permanent architectural complexity.
Preserve reusable structure preferentially.
