# 24. THE GENERAL THEORY
A medium is an encoding.
A representation is a structured account.
A generative grammar is a system of rules
that can produce valid representations.
Meaning is a contextual interpretation
of those representations and relationships.
Intelligence provides a means of inferring
plausible hidden structure from incomplete evidence.
The canonical compiler connects them.
INPUT
  -> OBSERVATION
  -> STRUCTURE
  -> MEANING
  -> GENERATIVE GRAMMAR
  -> TARGET INSTANTIATION
  -> EVALUATION
  -> RECONSTRUCTION
These arrows need not operate in only
one direction.
Any sufficiently constrained layer may
help reconstruct another.
However, not all mappings are unique,
not all information is recoverable,
and not all translations preserve
the same properties.
Therefore:
Preserve evidence.
Preserve relationships.
Preserve uncertainty.
Infer missing structure.
Test the inference.
Repair discrepancies.
Retain reusable rules.
